Comments for authors
--------------------
I liked reading your paper. I have the following comments:

C1: In relation to W1, can we use a bloom filter to identify dependent loads? I can think of a scheme where only violating (dependent) loads are added to the filter. This filter can be accessed after instructions are decoded, and independent loads can bypass MDP just like your scheme. This filtering scheme obviates profiling and ISA changes, and therefore is naturally more robust. 

In my opinion, a comparison to such an online filtering scheme, or a careful rebuttal against it if you think it is infeasible/impractical, would allow me to better-judge the benefit of your scheme. It should also improve the quality of your paper in general.

C2: You only show MDP performance with a Store Sets predictor. In my opinion, for small cores, a simple scheme, where loads are always speculated, may work well. 

C3: Memory order violations are sensitive to issue queue's scheduling policy/aggressiveness. I missed this parameter in the paper.

Questions/Issues for the authors to address in the rebuttal/revision
--------------------------------------------------------------------
In relation to C1, can you please provide a comparison to an online filtering scheme, or a careful rebuttal against it?

In relation to C2, can you please add results for the scheme where loads are always speculated? Note this scheme obviates MDP, and therefore is even more lucrative for area-constrained cores.

In relation to C3, can you please explain how ready instructions are scheduled by the issue queue?



