Comments for authors
--------------------
Please see other sections of this review.  Here are some additional comments / revisions:

- Section 2.2 (e.g., lines 241 and 242): Use the term "dispatch" instead of "issue" in this discussion.  Other parts of the paper use dispatch to mean insertion into IQ, LQ/SQ, and ROB, and issue to mean issuing from the IQ to the execution lanes.  The SSIT and LFST (or other MDP) are consulted in the frontend before the IQ, in order to synchronize stores-stores and store-loads via store inum tags (or a dependency matrix) in the IQ.  This is conveyed by the authors in the caption of Fig. 2.  Line 247: just don't use "issued" in this sentence, as the periodic clearing may be based on dispatched, issued, or retired instructions... I'd use dispatched or retired, over issued, if I were you.  Issue is buried in the complex OOO backend and execution lanes.  Dispatched is close to where the MDP exists.

- It is customary, and likely mathematically more "correct" (see H&P textbook), to use harmonic mean for averaging IPCs in Fig. 4.  Arithmetic mean for quantities (time, cycles, CPI, joules), harmonic mean for rates (IPC, IPS, Watts), geometric mean for ratios and speedups.  Harmonic mean of IPC converts IPCs to CPIs, then arithmetic mean of CPI, then overall reciprocal of that to convert back to IPC... so it's the equivalent of an arithmetic mean transformation for rates.

Questions/Issues for the authors to address in the rebuttal/revision
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Summary of requested revisions: (1) quantitative comparison with [16], (2) MDP and whole-core area savings w.r.t. iso-performance 64-entry and 128-entry SSITs, (3) performance impact of hint bytes or scavenge ARM/x86, (4) details about RISC-V mods and compiler/assembler, (5) two things under "Comments for authors".

1. The primary contribution over [16] appears to be *profiling* and *95-percentile store distance* instead of *static analysis* and *always-independent* (<- authors, please confirm or correct this).  I haven't read [16], but the abstract conveys the identical objective of marking loads as independent in order to avoid unnecessary MDP lookups and false dependencies.  Given the main idea is already published, this would seem to demand a quantitative comparison with [16].

Section 5 (Related Work) says of [16]: "...*Furthermore, due to relying on static analysis it also sees notable performance regressions elsewhere*..."  I would have expected static analysis to not hurt performance w.r.t. baseline, due to needing to prove independence (no false positives).  Is the regression due to per-function static analysis, and not global program analysis?  Or because of dynamically linked libraries?

Abstract of [16]:
"*This paper explores the potential of communicating information gained by static analysis from compilers to Out-of-Order (OoO) machines, focusing on the memory dependence predictor (MDP). The MDP enables loads to issue without all in-flight store addresses being known, with minimal memory order violations. We use LLVM to find loads with no dependencies and label them via their opcode. These labelled loads skip making lookups into the MDP, improving prediction accuracy by reducing false dependencies. We communicate this information in a minimally intrusive way, i.e. without introducing additional hardware costs or instruction bandwidth, providing these improvements without any additional overhead in the CPU. We find that across pure C/++ Spec2017 workloads, a significant number of load instructions can skip interacting with the MDP and lead to a performance gain. These results point to greater possibilities for static analysis as a source of near zero cost performance gains in future CPU designs.*"

2. The primary use-case is area-constrained cores, yet area savings is not reported.  For iso-performance (discussed shortly) and the small core, compare MDP area savings and whole-core area savings.  I'd expect the whole-core area savings to be minute.  Note that the *64-entry SSIT with hints* is iso-performance with the *128-entry SSIT without hints* (Fig. 4), and both are close to 1024-entry SSIT.  This will be ~2x area savings (maybe higher, maybe lower) in the MDP, but perhaps insignificant when considering whole-core area, even for this small core.

3. The hints are free in this paper due to using RISC-V, which has room for extensions (4 hint instruction opcodes, for example).  A complete paper would show the performance impact of increasing static and dynamic instruction count for fixed-length ISAs like ARM (could be quite high overhead) or adding a byte per labeled load for variable length ISAs.  For a revision, I would recommend either (1) quantifying the performance impact of adding 1 byte (x86) or 4 bytes (ARM) per labeled load (unsure how to append a single bit in the context of byte-addressable memory systems...) or (2) showing how ARM and/or x86 can be scavenged for labeling loads.

4. Please explain/clarify two missing items: (1) The RISC-V ISA modification. (2) Clarify whether there is an actual profiling compiler (and is it LLVM, gcc, or other?) and an assembler that emits labeled loads in the program binary, as opposed to a hypothetical toolchain and doing everything with simulator infrastructure (use simulation tools to profile loads, create a simulator input file of labeled loads, simulator reads in the labeled-loads input file into a simulator data structure, this data structure is consulted in the Decode stage).

5. Address the text and Fig. 4 (harmonic mean IPC) revisions under "Comments for authors".

----

Questions:

6. Have you explored filtering stores from the MDP as well, or are you concerned more about false positives with them?



