Pre-Rebuttal Overall merit
--------------------------
4. Major revision -- Fair work but with concerns. Satisfactory revision is
   critical for acceptance in the program

Comments for authors
--------------------
The paper was easy to follow and the mechanism is simple and indeed storage-wise cheap. Identifying independent loads to avoid unnecessary waits due to aliasing in the store sets table on an area-constrained core sounds reasonable.

Here are some of the concerns I have.

I think the performance gain is marginal for what it needs to do: instrumentation, profiling run, recompilation, ISA extension and hardware modification. A comparison against cost-effective pure hardware solutions I think are necessary to justify the changes.

The paper currently considers XS store sets and PHAST, but we can find cheap alternatives in the literature. For instance Alpha 21264's load wait table and store vectors (esp the tagged SVT in TACO paper) to name a few. Comparing against these techniques with equivalent hardware budget would strengthen the paper's claim.

The profiling part misses important details. Conventional PGO techniques collects program-level information or microarchitectural stats via performance counters or alike. This paper seems to require store distance (in SQ slots), a microarchitectural quantity that requires simulator access of the target core? The paper does not say what tool produces the profile or how this would ship in a real scenario. If the authors approximate store distance with dynamic instruction distance or something, this should be stated.

I'm not sure if SPEC CPU2017 intspeed is the right workload mix. These are compute-bound (mostly C/C++ I guess) and relatively easy case for PGO and MDP. Small efficiency cores actually run more: JIT'd JavaScript (e.g. V8 on Speedometer), interpreters (e.g. CPython on pyperformance), mobile ML inference (e.g. MLPerf Tiny). I guess JIT'd code is hard for any profile-guided technique. I think the paper would be stronger if it would evaluate on these workloads.

I think an additional experiment to demonstrate that a single global store distance threshold suffices would be nice to have.



